<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03Y5">Remark <span data-tag="03Y5">8.1.1.5</span>.</a> Let $\kappa $ be an uncountable cardinal. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a $\kappa $-small simplicial set, then $\operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is also $\kappa $-small. To prove this, we may assume without loss of generality that $\kappa $ is the smallest uncountable cardinal for which $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\kappa $-small. In particular, $\kappa $ is regular. It will therefore suffice to show that, for every integer $n$, the set of $n$-simplices of $\operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is $\kappa $-small (Proposition <a data-tag="03SA" href="/tag/03SA">4.9.4.10</a>). This follows from the $\kappa $-smallness of the set of $(2n+1)$-simplices of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (Remark <a data-tag="03JH" href="/tag/03JH">8.1.1.2</a>). </p>
